## 3. Systemic patterns swept (instances changed)

1. **"ter" as the default "and"** (a8; heaviest in 003/004/007, also 005/006/008/009).
   410 automatic replacements across those 7 files, 20 of them backed off again to stay inside the
   `english` gate (see §4), then 45 hand fixes so that "in" sits at the inner level and "ter" at the outer
   one (including 10 block-initial "ter" in 006/008 that the automatic pass could not reach).
   Remaining "ter" per file: 001 23 · 002 19 · 003 16 · 004 37 · 005 1 · 006 0 · 007 64 · 008 0 · 009 0 ·
   010 10 · 011 14 · 012 2 · 013 0 · 014 13 · 015 0 · 016 21 · 017 16 · 018 11.
   (001/002/010–018 were already within normal Slovene ratios and were not swept; the residue in 003/004/007
   is forced by the `english` gate — see §4.)
2. **"smeti" standing in for epistemic "lahko"** (a4, a7, a21, a25, a53). 77 of 112 occurrences rewritten
   (002 17, 003 17, 004 2, 007 33, 008 5, 014 1, 016 2). The 35 left are genuine deontic uses
   ("ne sme presegati", "ne smejo prosto dvigovati", "poklici, ki jih smejo opravljati", …).
3. **Statute citations in English order** (a40 and the a-verdict systemic row). ~48 citations rewritten to
   the Slovene order ("Po 371. členu …", "Prvi odstavek 1052. člena …", "4. odstavek 15-1. člena …"):
   002 6, 003 4, 004 16, 007 9, 011 1, 012 1, 014 11. Two constraints shaped the wording:
   a sentence may not *start* with "N. člen" (markdown would read it as an ordered-list item and the
   `blocks` check counts list items — this actually fired on 004), so those were recast as
   "Po N. členu …" / "Določba N. člena …"; and the 012 source-list label had to keep the source's own order
   (see §4).
